#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100638 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3433cbc0-fe8c-5b67-a888-4df13b4b68c3" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Derivative of a Smooth Function"}] :question/id #uuid "c6ce8503-265f-5a02-93f4-ad99390c2734" :question/math-academy-id "e-333" :question/problem "The graph of the function $f(x)$ is shown below. Sketch the graph of $f'(x)$.\n\n![](images/d9/d9746bb18a0e3ae662e477173e4da8b6599245edb02003287d83ab5e894aa814.png)" :question/worked-solution "Notice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have $f'(x)=0$.\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have $f'(x)>0$.\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have $f'(x) < 0$.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary points were identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\textrm{max}$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{min}$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n| $f'$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nAdditionally, the function has an inflection point at\n\n$$\nx \\approx 2\n$$\n\nSo\n\n$$\nf''(2)\\approx0\n$$\n\nand the tangent to the graph of $y=f'(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis.\n\nWe don't know the exact value of $f'(2)$ but from the graph of $y=f(x)$, we can conclude that at\n\n$$\nx \\approx 2\n$$\n\nthe slope is negative.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y=f'(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/a8/a8f8c622296689efa9290c0ecb219abdba20cf71052ca77df1a131658d534a68.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100639 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100826 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2ef7a61-79b9-5464-ac2a-3afb7dddd1c2" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Second Derivative of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "68f7ad36-cab7-5ffe-99d9-e720bbf7971a" :question/math-academy-id "e-334" :question/problem "The graph of the function $y=f'(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is shown below. Sketch the graph of $y=f''(x)$.\n\n![](images/bf/bf8bde9f9b5060fbf14a329833f35ce9970c850e805e0c551cccaa70d6288c06.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, if we denote\n\n$$\ng(x) = f'(x)\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf''(x)=g'(x)\n$$\n\nSo, we need to sketch the graph of $y=g'(x)$ given the graph of $y=g(x)$, as usual.\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- At the critical points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have $g'(x)=0$.\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have $g'(x)>0$.\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have $g'(x) < 0$.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the critical points were identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=g(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $g$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{not extr.}$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{min}$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n| $g'$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nAdditionally, the function has an inflection point at\n\n$$\nx \\approx 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nx\\approx 2.25\n$$\n\nAt the inflection points, we have $g''(x)=0$, so the tangent to the graph of $y=g'(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis at\n\n$$\nx\\approx 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nx\\approx 2.25\n$$\n\nWe don't know the exact value of $g'(1)$ and $g'(2.25)$, but from the graph of $y=g(x)$, we can conclude that at\n\n$$\nx \\approx 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nx\\approx 2.25\n$$\n\nthe slope is zero and negative, respectively.\n\nTherefore, the graph of\n\n$$\ny=g'(x)=f''(x)\n$$\n\nmay look as shown below.\n\n![](images/78/785b221ae245b389e707f8189c91e57af888ecbb44dfce384ab1c60958d91a6f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100762 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100929 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa025d7b-74d5-5627-a7cd-12e3681b8b82" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Derivative of a Quadratic Function"}] :question/id #uuid "98a83d94-0f65-5475-850e-c1abd8836e15" :question/math-academy-id "e-6819" :question/problem "Consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ below. Sketch the graph of $y=f'(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$.\n\n![](images/1f/1fcbb5677ad7c8f630ccd7d194ef92f59fe3424d6bdb85e20f1ae522567b94ec.png)" :question/worked-solution "Notice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have $f'(x)=0$.\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have $f'(x)>0$.\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have $f'(x) < 0$.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary point was identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{min}$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n| $f'$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nAdditionally, from the graph of $y=f(x)$, we can conclude that there are no inflection points. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is concave up in $(-\\infty,\\infty)$. So, $f''(x) > 0$ and $y=f'(x)$ is increasing.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y=f'(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/15/1567dcdd3dac0eb6b76b71e20466a77457a3d956c85be96048e5d3a94568c03a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100763 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100930 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "97710476-c327-5b2d-9675-10f36b967d3a" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Derivative of a Piecewise-Differentiable Function"}] :question/id #uuid "0b633f45-0fdb-5d4b-90e0-51c6628ce360" :question/math-academy-id "e-6820" :question/problem "Consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ below. Sketch the graph of $y=f'(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$.\n\n![](images/ed/ed26d7560d9bd7294bf35a8e0e52292a7b5922d419303c63430c73c32c63070e.png)" :question/worked-solution "Notice the following:\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have $f'(x)>0$.\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we get $f'(x) < 0$.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\textrm{max}$ | $\\searrow$ |\n| $f'$ | $+$ | $\\textrm{undef.}$ | $-$ |\n\nFrom the graph of $y=f(x)$, we can conclude that there are no inflection points. Also:\n\n- For $x \\in (-\\infty,-1)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ is an increasing straight line. So, $y=f'(x)$ will be a positive constant on this interval.\n- For $x \\in (-1,\\infty)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ is concave down. So, $f''(x) < 0$ and $y=f'(x)$ is decreasing on this interval.\n\nAdditionally, we have a rapid change of the slope of the tangent line when we pass through $x=-1$ from left to right. This means that the derivative is not defined at $x=-1$, and we obtain a jump discontinuity at this point for the graph of $y=f'(x)$.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y=f'(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/d0/d00aadf7b1666ea8a762833d1844d638a224759c58d1b06929657ddb90dc4abb.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101301 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3433cbc0-fe8c-5b67-a888-4df13b4b68c3" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Derivative of a Smooth Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2effdf9-1172-58d4-b628-4c933cc6ca44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2f/2f85d0d358a179eb7be4e13bd9d8a8d787e48c24971fd3f65d1efb38859c5a32.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099128} {:answer/id #uuid "ed5bf606-62bc-5e83-a623-1d665ec7536d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7a/7adc74d7e58a8b8a259db58189a09ccf02deafaa8882d7bfd093ad318da9f40a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099129} {:answer/id #uuid "058cd11c-3503-5168-a848-aa5e9e4b6340" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/14/141efeb601a2cd56f51a3e2262d96548ab77261e89d70150b60e3805b6ca07c4.p